.TH CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT \- zero-initialiser for chttpsvr_config_t with sensible defaults
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Expands to a compound literal that initialises a chttpsvr_config_t
with the following defaults:

  host                  = "0.0.0.0"
  port                  = 8080
  max_body_size         = 4 MiB
  read_timeout_ms       = 0   (no timeout; use facil.io default ~40 s)
  idle_timeout_ms       = 0   (0 = fall back to read_timeout_ms)
  stream_read_timeout_ms = 30000   (30 s; 0 = wait indefinitely)
  max_body_read_duration_ms = 0   (disabled; no cap on total read time)
  tls                   = NULL
  worker_thread_count   = 0   (0 = CPU core count)
  worker_queue_capacity = 0   (0 = 1024 * worker_thread_count)

stream_read_timeout_ms bounds how long a worker thread will wait for
the *next* batch of body bytes while reading a request body (buffered
or streaming), independent of read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms; those
reset on any connection activity and so do not protect against a
client that trickles bytes just fast enough to always beat them.  On
expiry, chttpsvr_req_read returns -1 and chttpsvr_req_stream_error
reports ccol_timed_out; buffered routes respond 408 automatically.

Note that stream_read_timeout_ms itself resets on any new byte too, so
a client that trickles a byte or two just before each gap expires
defeats it the same way it defeats read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms.
max_body_read_duration_ms closes that loophole by separately capping
the *total* wall-clock time spent reading one request's body,
regardless of how many individual gaps it took to get there.  It is
disabled (0) by default so existing deployments are unaffected unless
they opt in.  On expiry it reports the same ccol_timed_out outcome as
a stream_read_timeout_ms expiry.

Use CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED for worker_queue_capacity to create a pool
with no capacity limit.

Override individual fields after applying the macro:

  chttpsvr_config_t cfg = CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT;
  cfg.port = 9090;
  cfg.worker_thread_count = 8;
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED (3)
